WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.027
Presenting eval results honestly.

2
00:00:02.307 --> 00:00:08.067
This is where reports most often go wrong, and where your Studio 3 training is a real advantage.

3
00:00:08.347 --> 00:00:19.099
Evan Miller’s paper on adding error bars to evals, and Anthropic’s companion post, make the case that an eval score is an estimate from a sample, and should be reported like one.

4
00:00:19.379 --> 00:00:21.384
Six rules follow for the capstone.

5
00:00:21.664 --> 00:00:23.776
Every number has n and an interval.

6
00:00:24.056 --> 00:00:29.581
“74 percent” alone tells a reader nothing about how much it would move on another sample.

7
00:00:29.861 --> 00:00:36.539
“74 percent (95 percent interval 65 to 82, n=100)” does.

8
00:00:36.819 --> 00:00:38.163
Comparisons are paired.

9
00:00:38.443 --> 00:00:44.480
Report the per-case difference against the baseline with its interval, not two separate scores side by side.

10
00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.176
Two overlapping intervals can still hide a clear paired difference, and the reverse.

11
00:00:49.456 --> 00:00:50.971
The headline is held-out.

12
00:00:51.251 --> 00:00:53.427
Dev numbers are what you tuned against.

13
00:00:53.707 --> 00:00:57.632
Report them, but lead with held-out, and say how many times you ran it.

14
00:00:57.912 --> 00:00:59.235
Disclose the variants.

15
00:00:59.515 --> 00:01:03.333
If you tried twelve prompt versions and report the best, say twelve.

16
00:01:03.613 --> 00:01:09.117
The more variants you try against the same cases, the more likely the best one looks good by chance.

17
00:01:09.397 --> 00:01:10.827
Words match the interval.

18
00:01:11.107 --> 00:01:16.461
If the interval excludes zero, you may say the system is better, by an amount in that range.

19
00:01:16.741 --> 00:01:20.752
If it includes zero, say you could not distinguish them, and give the range.

20
00:01:21.032 --> 00:01:26.344
Avoid “significant” unless you mean a stated test, because readers hear it as “important.

21
00:01:26.624 --> 00:01:34.005
” For a cheaper model, the honest claim is equivalence within a tolerance you set in advance: the whole interval sits inside it.

22
00:01:34.285 --> 00:01:35.715
Translate for the summary.

23
00:01:35.995 --> 00:01:45.488
“About 3 in 4 answers fully correct, and we’d expect between roughly 2 in 3 and 4 in 5 on new questions like these” is accurate and readable.

24
00:01:45.768 --> 00:01:48.904
Use points, not percent, for differences between rates.
